---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const faqSchema = {
 "@context": "https://schema.org",
 "@type": "FAQPage",
 "mainEntity": [
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Does a CDN host my website?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "No: it caches and serves copies. Your origin server or hosting remains the source of truth."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Why is my origin IP still visible?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Direct connections to historical IPs, subdomains not proxied, or mail records revealing infrastructure. Full origin hiding requires deliberate configuration."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Do CDNs work with dynamic content?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Yes: dynamic requests are not cached but still benefit from optimized routes between edge and origin."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is cache poisoning then?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "An attack on the cache itself: tricking the edge into storing attacker-controlled responses. Reputable CDNs defend against it; keep cache keys conservative."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Do I need a CDN with static hosting?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Static hosts on global platforms already behave like CDNs; a second layer is usually redundant."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "How do I purge the cache after deploying?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Every CDN has a purge API/dashboard action; versioned asset filenames make most purges unnecessary."
   }
  }
 ]
}
---
<BaseLayout
  title="What Is a CDN and How Does It Work?"
  description="CDNs explained: how edge networks cache and serve your site globally, why DNS routes users to the nearest node, and what changes for your DNS records."
  schema={[faqSchema]}
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/ip-and-networking/" class="hover:text-accent">IP & Networking</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">What Is a CDN</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">What Is a CDN and How Does It Work?</h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">A Content Delivery Network stores copies of your site at hundreds of global locations (edge nodes) and serves each visitor from the closest one. The result: lower latency, absorbed traffic spikes, and an attack surface that is the CDN's problem instead of yours.</p>
    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">The mechanics</h2>
      <ol class="list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-5"><li>You point your DNS at the CDN: either an A record to their anycast IPs or a CNAME to their hostname (flattened at the apex).</li><li>A visitor resolves your domain; DNS answers with the nearest edge node’s address.</li><li>The edge serves cached content instantly; misses fetch from your origin and cache per your rules.</li><li>Dynamic requests pass through the edge to your origin over optimized routes.</li></ol>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">What it changes for you</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5"><li><strong class="text-ink">Your origin IP hides</strong> behind the CDN: an uptime and security win, and the reason A records point at edge IPs.</li><li><strong class="text-ink">Cache rules become configuration:</strong> what to cache, for how long, and what always passes through (APIs, logged-in pages).</li><li><strong class="text-ink">TLS is terminated at the edge:</strong> certificates are managed by the CDN or uploaded by you.</li><li><strong class="text-ink">Purge control matters:</strong> deploys need cache invalidation or versioned asset names.</li></ul>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">CDN vs no CDN</h2>
      <p>A personal blog on a single-region server gains global speed and DDoS absorption. An already-fast site gains resilience more than speed. What no site gains: freedom from correct DNS, because the CDN is only reachable if your records point at it reliably. Verify changes with the <a href="/dns-propagation-checker/">propagation checker</a>; CDN cutovers live and die on DNS.</p>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3"><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Does a CDN host my website?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">No: it caches and serves copies. Your origin server or hosting remains the source of truth.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Why is my origin IP still visible?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Direct connections to historical IPs, subdomains not proxied, or mail records revealing infrastructure. Full origin hiding requires deliberate configuration.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Do CDNs work with dynamic content?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Yes: dynamic requests are not cached but still benefit from optimized routes between edge and origin.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What is cache poisoning then?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">An attack on the cache itself: tricking the edge into storing attacker-controlled responses. Reputable CDNs defend against it; keep cache keys conservative.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Do I need a CDN with static hosting?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Static hosts on global platforms already behave like CDNs; a second layer is usually redundant.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">How do I purge the cache after deploying?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Every CDN has a purge API/dashboard action; versioned asset filenames make most purges unnecessary.</p></details></div>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Related</h2>
      <ul class="mt-3 grid gap-2"><li><a href="/manage-dns-in-cloudflare/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">Cloudflare DNS-only vs proxied →</a></li><li><a href="/cname-flattening/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">CNAME flattening →</a></li><li><a href="/dns-propagation-checker/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">DNS propagation checker →</a></li></ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
